---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const waitlist =
  'mailto:support@hootventures.ai?subject=Remy%20waitlist&body=Hi%2C%20I%27d%20like%20early%20access%20to%20Remy.%0A%0AName%3A%20%0AiPhone%20model%3A%20';
---

<Layout
  title="Why I built Remy · Remy"
  description="A note from the founder: the conflict of interest at the heart of personal AI, and the five things Remy was built to get right."
>
  <header class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-6 pt-8 sm:pt-12">
    <nav class="flex items-center justify-between">
      <a href="/" class="flex items-center gap-2">
        <img src="/remy-mark.png" alt="" class="h-7 w-7" />
        <span class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Remy</span>
      </a>
      <a
        href={waitlist}
        class="inline-flex h-10 items-center rounded-full bg-[var(--ink)] px-5 text-sm font-semibold text-white"
        >Get early access</a
      >
    </nav>
  </header>

  <main class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-6 py-16 sm:py-20">
    <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.06em] text-[var(--muted)]">
      A note from the founder
    </p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">Why I built Remy</h1>

    <div class="letter mt-9">
      <p>
        I loved ChatGPT from the first week. I used it constantly. But when it came to the
        personal stuff - my kid&rsquo;s school, a health question, plans with my wife - I never
        quite felt comfortable sharing personal information.
      </p>

      <p>
        That feeling never went away, and eventually I understood why. AI model companies make
        their models better by using as much data as possible. So a company that is in the
        business of building AI models also having all my personal data did not make me feel
        safe - whether I checked the &ldquo;don&rsquo;t use my data to train AI&rdquo; box or
        not. <b
          >To me, there is a conflict of interest between my needs and their business
          objective.</b
        >
      </p>

      <p>So I went looking for an alternative.</p>

      <p>
        There are some, but they are built for very technical people: install this, configure
        that, run your own server. I could never get my wife or my teenager to use a tool like
        that. It was clear to me that what I wanted did not exist yet.
      </p>

      <p>That is when I started working on Remy.</p>

      <p>The things I cared about were simple:</p>

      <p><b>I want my data used for one purpose and one purpose alone: for me.</b></p>

      <p>
        <b>I want it to be so simple that my whole family can use it</b> - my wife, my teenager,
        and even my almost 80 year old mom.
      </p>

      <p>
        <b>I want it to reach out to me when something needs my attention,</b> like a real
        assistant would. Not the other way around.
      </p>

      <p>
        <b>I want it to be an extension of my brain,</b> so I don&rsquo;t have to remember
        everything about everything all the time.
      </p>

      <p>
        <b>And I want it to be able to do things in the real world</b> - but only with my
        approval, never by itself.
      </p>

      <p>
        This thinking shaped how Remy feels. ChatGPT, Gemini, and others have trained us to
        start a new chat for every new topic. But that is not how you work with a real
        assistant. With Remy, it is just one chat: you talk to it like you would talk to a
        person. You can still start a new chat for a special project, or a place to keep your
        recipes. But you never have to think about where a thought goes. You just tell Remy.
      </p>

      <p>
        What makes Remy different is in our approach to how we make money and how your data is
        used:
      </p>

      <p>
        <b>We only make money one way: people paying for Remy.</b> No ads. No selling data to
        third parties. If we break your trust, we lose your business.
      </p>

      <p>
        <b>We are not in the business of building AI models.</b> When Remy uses an AI model to
        think, that request is processed and never kept. Every AI provider Remy uses is held to
        a zero data retention standard.
      </p>

      <p>
        <b>Nothing real happens without your approval.</b> Remy cannot send an email, make a
        call, or book anything on its own. You press and hold to approve, or it does not happen.
      </p>

      <p>
        <b>You can delete everything, any time.</b> If you decide to delete Remy, delete it all
        right in the app. We plan to add a way to download your data too. We want you to stay
        because Remy is great, not because your data is stuck with us.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is the future I want for personal AI - an extension of us, for us, and us alone. We
        use Remy every day. Even my wife loves it. I hope you give it a try and tell us how to
        make it better for you.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 border-t border-[var(--divider)] pt-7">
      <p class="text-lg font-bold">Rohit</p>
      <p class="mt-0.5 text-lg font-bold">Founder, Remy</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-14 text-center">
      <a
        href={waitlist}
        class="inline-flex h-[52px] items-center gap-2 rounded-full bg-[var(--ink)] px-8 text-[17px] font-semibold text-white"
        >Get early access - free</a
      >
      <p class="mt-4 text-[13.5px] text-[var(--muted)]">
        iPhone &middot; free during beta &middot; sign in with Google, nothing to configure
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>

  <footer class="mt-12 border-t border-[var(--line)]">
    <div
      class="mx-auto flex max-w-2xl items-center justify-between px-6 py-8 text-sm text-[var(--muted)]"
    >
      <a class="hover:text-[var(--ink)]" href="/">&larr; Back to home</a>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6">
        <a class="hover:text-[var(--ink)]" href="/security">Security</a>
        <a class="hover:text-[var(--ink)]" href="/privacy">Privacy</a>
        <a class="hover:text-[var(--ink)]" href="/tos">Terms</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </footer>
</Layout>

<style>
  .letter p {
    margin-top: 22px;
    font-size: 17.5px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .letter p:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .letter p b {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 700;
  }
</style>
